 After each revised-PROF publication, retain only a concise operational handover: task goal and exact user constraints, repository and verified commit, frozen baseline/source identities, active material, queue counts, remaining tasks, ownership and unresolved access limits. Start the next author and reader with fork_turns: "none". A fresh generation coordinator, when used, receives only that handover and original inputs. Keep old teaching, diagnoses, reader reports, proposed answers and detailed author reasoning outside new generation inputs; preserved audit evidence may be inspected separately after the new document is frozen. Do not claim to erase the current agent's live context or pretraining. Where no context-erasure control exists, use a new agent with no inherited conversation and describe that actual boundary.
 
-For Jonathan's run, the complete root skill package on the current main branch of alverstris/PROF-Skill.md- is the source of truth. Fetch and verify it before generation; an unpublished local candidate or an older cached copy cannot supply the operative PROF. On his computer, overwrite C:/Users/alver/.codex/skills/prof with the complete package from the pinned published commit at each iteration's start and after every revised-PROF publication, before fresh generation. Follow the staged replacement, backup and installed-byte verification in [the SASIS local synchronisation step](references/sasis.md#synchronise-the-installed-prof-from-github), then reread the actual installed skill. Jonathan explicitly authorised this download/overwrite on 2026-10-14; it supersedes the earlier prohibition on updating his Windows installation. In an environment without access to that installation, record synchronisation as unavailable and use the verified published package without claiming a local update. Preserve his product/format request: skill self-iteration does not itself request a PDF or workbook. Use repo-native teaching and evidence artifacts when no other iteration format was requested. A protocol revision is preparation, not evidence that a lecture has been evaluated.
